    <!-- "About ..." explanations on the metric screen -->
    <string name="dt_about_wind">Wind speed is the average over 10 minutes at 10 m above ground. Gusts are short bursts that can be much stronger. Direction is where the wind comes FROM: a north wind blows from north to south.</string>
    <string name="dt_about_humidity">Relative humidity is how much water vapour the air holds compared to the maximum at that temperature. The dew point is a better measure of how muggy it feels: the higher it is, the stickier the air.</string>
    <string name="dt_about_uv">The UV index measures the strength of sunburn-causing ultraviolet radiation. It peaks around solar noon and is higher at altitude, near water, snow and sand. Clouds reduce it, but not always much.</string>
    <string name="dt_about_feels">Feels-like (apparent) temperature combines air temperature with wind, humidity and sunshine to estimate how the weather feels on your skin.</string>
    <string name="dt_about_pressure">Air pressure at sea level. Standard pressure is 1013 hPa. Highs usually bring stable, settled weather; lows bring clouds, wind and rain. Rapid falls are a sign of an approaching storm.</string>
    <string name="dt_about_visibility">Horizontal distance at which objects can be clearly seen. Below 1 km is fog; below 10 km usually means mist, haze or precipitation.</string>
    <string name="dt_about_precip">Total rain, showers and snow (as water) per hour. The percentage is the chance that at least 0.1 mm falls in that hour, combined from several weather models.</string>
    <string name="dt_about_cloud">Fraction of the sky covered by clouds at all heights. Low clouds block the most sun; high thin clouds can still let a lot of sunshine through.</string>
    <string name="dt_about_sun">Sunrise and sunset are when the top edge of the sun touches the horizon. Daylight is the time between them; sunshine is the time with direct sun not hidden by clouds.</string>
    <string name="dt_about_moon">The moon goes through a full cycle of phases every 29.5 days. Phases are calculated on your device, so they work offline.</string>
    <string name="dt_about_air">The Air Quality Index turns pollutant concentrations into one number. It is based on the Copernicus Atmosphere Monitoring Service (CAMS) model, not a local sensor, so very local sources may not show up.</string>
    <string name="dt_about_sea">Wave height is the significant wave height: the average of the highest third of waves, roughly what an experienced observer would estimate. Single waves can be up to twice as high. Swell is made by distant storms; wind waves by local wind. Data: Open-Meteo Marine (ECMWF, Météo-France, DWD and Copernicus Marine wave and ocean models).</string>
    <string name="dt_about_pollen">Pollen concentration in grains per cubic metre of air, from the CAMS European pollen model. Sensitive people may notice symptoms from around 10–20 grains/m³ for grass and birch.</string>

